 @raawesome3851  
Upscaling is upscaling and every card will see a performance boost, the subjective differences like with turned down settings will be acceptable to different people at different levels.
The main consideration is how often you either can or will use a feature and DLSS is only a sometimes available technology and as it's proprietary will likely only last as long as there's no universal competing technology. Historically proprietary solutions have a short life before being supplanted by standardised technologies (e.g. G-sync compatible which is basically freesync and not proprietary). As it is DLSS is a niche solution utilising specific hardware that's not required for gaming or even raytracing as RTX denoises with CUDA. Even RTX voice can be made to work on Pascal cards showing that tensor cores are there just because of Pro compute card requirements in tensorflow workloads and DLSS is just taking advantage of them, even though DirectML doesn't have the latency overheads associated with DLSS and tensor.

