 @tarkitarker0815  Unless it was DOA within the return window, the store you bought it from is going to tell you to go to the manufacturer.

And you also assume the person knows the problem. If your computer won't boot, you need to figure out if it is your CPU, PSU, GPU, RAM, or MB and then deal with the manufacturer RMA process. If you have a warranty from a system integrator you tell them your system isn't working and they figure out why and then deal with the manufacturer.

Yes it may well take longer from finding the problem to having a working system again, but you don't need the knowledge to troubleshoot or to invest your time in troubleshooting.

 @tarkitarker0815  And if it stops working after seven months?

As to MB error codes etc, that can tell you where to look, but doesn't necessarily isolate the problem. Sure, it may tell you the memory isn't being detected, but that could be issues with the CPU, MB, or memory or potentially issues in the BIOS or something. And for a lot of people figuring that out isn't worth their time or effort.

The simple fact is that it may not make sense for you, but you are not most people. Most people wouldn't know what to do if you offered them $100 to remove the RAM from a PC and it isn't worth their time and effort to learn that if they aren't interested in it.

